ffe9eebe-5279-476c-87e9-c56544ecaec2	a7409952-18ab-47e0-a7ab-a5b53426343f	2	Clay Monster Friends	Children roll, pat, and pinch clay to make a body, then use loose parts like pipe cleaners, googly eyes, and feathers to bring their own unique monster to life.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Roll a clay ball, pinch it into a monster body, press in colorful googly eyes, and add pipe-cleaner legs and feather wings.", "Together": "Squish and squeeze the clay together to warm it up, making funny roaring sounds as we stretch it out.", "Challenge": "Can you make a silly clay monster with lots of eyes or wiggly legs?", "Materials": ["Air-dry clay (or play dough)", "Large googly eyes", "Colorful pipe cleaners cut into 3-inch pieces", "Bright craft feathers", "Short colorful craft sticks"], "Reflection": "What does your monster love to eat? How does it walk with those legs?", "ChildChoice": "Choosing which colors of feathers to use, how many eyes to add, and where to poke the pipe-cleaner legs.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The moment a child pushes a giant googly eye into their clay lump and suddenly laughs as their clay 'comes alive' as a character.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Proudly showing off their silly monster friend to the group and giving it a funny name.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "How many eyes does your monster need? Is your monster friendly or silly?", "Try": "If the feathers or pipe cleaners keep falling out, try poking a deep hole with your finger first, then tucking the item inside and pinching the clay shut around it.", "Watch": "Observe how children use their fingers to pinch and push. Watch to ensure younger children do not put small parts in their mouths.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Warm Up & Squeeze", "ChildrenDo": "Pinch, squish, pat, and roll their clay ball on the table to warm it up.", "TeacherDoes": "Model different hand actions like rolling, clapping the clay, and squeezing, making fun sound effects.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Shape the Monster", "ChildrenDo": "Pinch and push the clay to make a base shape for their monster (flat, tall, or round).", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to try pinching 'ears' or poking 'belly buttons' into their clay.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Add Features", "ChildrenDo": "Select googly eyes, pipe cleaners, and feathers and press them firmly into the clay.", "TeacherDoes": "Support fine-motor coordination by showing children how to press items deep into the clay so they stay secure.", "DurationMinutes": 8}, {"Name": "Monster Parade", "ChildrenDo": "Gently carry or point to their clay monsters and share their names and sounds.", "TeacherDoes": "Celebrate each unique monster creation, highlighting creative choices like 'Look at those three green legs!'", "DurationMinutes": 2}], "Safety": ["Supervise closely to prevent children from putting googly eyes or small clay pieces in their mouths.", "Ensure pipe cleaner ends are not sharp."], "Closing": "Let's give our monsters a gentle wave goodbye. We will leave them on our trays to dry so they can stay strong and play another day!", "Extensions": ["Take photos of the monsters and help children write down a one-sentence story about what their monster likes to do.", "Create a simple cardboard 'home' or cave for the monsters to play in."], "Adaptations": ["For 2-year-olds: Offer larger items like craft sticks and big blocks of clay that are easier to handle.", "For 4-year-olds: Challenge them to make a family of two monsters, one big and one small."], "Preparation": ["Portion the clay into child-sized balls (about the size of an apple).", "Cut pipe cleaners into short, safe lengths.", "Sort the googly eyes, feathers, and sticks into small, accessible cups."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place one ball of clay on each child's tray.", "Put the cups of loose parts (eyes, feathers, pipe cleaners) in the center of the table so they are easy to reach."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a low table so children have plenty of elbow room to roll and press.", "Use plastic trays or placemats to define each child's workspace."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Look at this squishy clay! It is so soft and ready to play. Today, we are going to use our strong hands to make our very own silly monster friends. Let's squeeze it!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child gets excited about putting ten eyes on one side of the clay, validate their unique vision by counting the eyes together with funny blinking noises.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 20}, "TeacherGuidance": "Focus on the sensory play and exploration. For 2 and 3-year-olds, poking holes and sticking items into the clay is the main developmental goal, not creating a perfectly shaped figure."}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 22:43:07.987427+00
