ur beautiful garden glow!'", "Extensions": ["Once dry, tape the colorful coffee-filter flowers to the classroom windows to create a 'stained-glass magic garden' that glows in the sunlight."], "Adaptations": ["For children with fine-motor challenges, swap the small pipettes for squeeze bottles or turkey basters which are easier to grasp."], "Preparation": ["Pre-cut coffee filters into fun flower shapes.", "Lightly dust the dry coffee filters with a thin layer of baking soda.", "Mix liquid watercolors with a splash of white vinegar in plastic cups (this creates the fizzy magic reaction)."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place baskets of blocks near the trays.", "Set out pipettes, cups of colorful liquids, and the baking-soda-dusted coffee filter flowers on small trays for each pair."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Arrange low tables or floor areas with large plastic trays to contain the block building and wet messes.", "Create 'Wizard Potion Stations' where cups of colored vinegar-water are easily accessible."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Welcome, apprentice Color Wizards! Oh no, the forest has lost its sparkle! We need to work in wizarding duos to build giant wooden flower stems and brew magical fizzy potions to make these sleeping flowers bloom again. Are you ready to splash some magic?", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Catch the moment two children laugh together as their colors bleed into each other on the filter, creating a brand new color they didn't expect.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Adopt a playful 'Head Wizard' persona! Instead of focusing on neatness, encourage mess, experimentation, and dramatic play. Help partners coordinate by giving them silly cooperative challenges, like 'Can you place a block at the exact same time?' or 'Mix your potions together to see what new magic you create!'"}	Initial Creation	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 21:34:35.264551+00
07486bef-6db7-4fbf-b710-b4c84260f70c	954d2948-70d8-4837-aebb-75dd0679591b	2	The Goofy Fizzy Flower Rescue	Apprentice wizards wear silly hats, chant funny spells, and work in giggly pairs to build wobbly block towers and wake up giant coffee-filter flowers with exploding, fizzing color potions!	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "A collaborative, colorful 3D garden of giant, fizzing watercolor flowers resting on top of goofy wooden block towers.", "Together": "Working in silly wizard duos, performing secret high-fives, and taking turns dripping color potions to trigger funny fizzing reactions together.", "Challenge": "Can you and your wizard partner build a wacky block tower and make your flower 'gasp' and fizz with magic colors?", "Materials": ["Wooden building blocks", "Coffee filters cut into simple flower shapes", "Baking soda (sprinkled on the dry filters beforehand)", "White vinegar mixed with bright liquid watercolors (Red, Blue, Yellow)", "Pipettes and small squeeze bottles ('Wizard Wands')", "Deep plastic trays or sensory bins (to contain the fizz)", "Silly paper wizard hats or headbands", "Sponges for cleanup play"], "Reflection": "Giggling together as they invent silly names for their new colors (like 'Snotty-Grape-Green' or 'Exploding-Banana-Yellow') and talking about whose tower wobbled the most.", "ChildChoice": "Choosing their own wizard tools (pipettes or squeeze bottles), picking wacky potion colors to mix, and deciding how tall or wobbly to build their block tower.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The instant eyes-wide-open laughter when the colored potion hits the baking soda, making a loud 'PSST!' sound as the colors explode and blend.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Striking a dramatic wizard pose with their partner next to their completed, colorful, fizzing creation while making victory wizard sounds.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "If your fizzy flower had a funny voice, what would it say to you right now? What silly name should we give this bubbly green color you made?", "Try": "If a child is hesitant to use the pipettes, pretend the pipette is a sleepy baby dragon that needs its tummy squeezed to breathe colored fire.", "Watch": "Watch for kids sharing giggles when the baking soda fizzes, and see how they playfully negotiate which colors to mix together on their shared flower.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Goofy Tower Building", "ChildrenDo": "Put on imaginary (or paper) wizard hats, invent a secret partner handshake, and build a silly, tall block tower inside their tray to act as a flower stem.", "TeacherDoes": "Guides children into pairs. Challenges them with playful prompts like 'Can you build using only your elbows?' or 'Make a funny squeak every time you add a block!'", "DurationMinutes": 6}, {"Name": "The Great Fizzy Tickle!", "ChildrenDo": "Place their coffee-filter flower on top of the tower. Squeeze and drip their colorful potions onto the flower, making loud 'PSST!' and 'FIZZ!' sounds as the color bubbles up and spreads.", "TeacherDoes": "Acts as the hyperactive Head Wizard, walking around to cheer on the bubble explosions and encouraging partners to mix their colors to see what goofy shades they create.", "DurationMinutes": 11}, {"Name": "The Wacky Garden Parade", "ChildrenDo": "March around the room like funny wizards, admiring everyone's fizzing towers and showing off their color blends with silly poses.", "TeacherDoes": "Leads the parade, pointing out the most wobbly towers, the brightest fizzes, and the best cooperative teamwork.", "DurationMinutes": 4}, {"Name": "Sponge Monster Cleanup", "ChildrenDo": "Pretend their sponges are 'Hungry Sponge Monsters' that love to eat colorful puddles, wiping up the trays and squeezing the liquid into a central bucket.", "TeacherDoes": "Makes funny eating noises ('OM NOM NOM!') to turn the cleanup process into a hilarious game.", "DurationMinutes": 4}], "Safety": ["Keep towels nearby for wet floors.", "Remind children to keep the vinegar potion away from their eyes and to wash hands if they get sticky."], "Closing": "Incredible wizarding! You have successfully tickled the flowers awake! Put your hands in the middle with your partner and shout: 'Abracadabra, fizzy-pop-shazam!'", "Extensions": ["Once dry, hang the goofy, colorful flowers on the windows and let the children draw funny monster faces or googly eyes on them with markers."], "Adaptations": ["For younger children or those with fine-motor challenges, swap the small pipettes for larger, easy-to-squeeze plastic condiment bottles."], "Preparation": ["Dust the coffee-filter flowers with a thin layer of baking soda.", "Mix the colored liquid watercolors with white vinegar in small cups.", "Place blocks and trays at each station."], "MaterialSetup": ["Put a basket of blocks, two cups of colored vinegar potion, two pipettes, and two dry baking-soda-dusted flowers inside each tray."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Set up the plastic trays on the floor or low tables so children can move around freely and play comfortably."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Alakazam, magical friends! The sleeping flowers are feeling super lazy today. We need to team up as Wobbly Wizards, build silly block towers, and tickle these flowers awake with our explosive, fizzy-wuzzy potions! Are you ready to make some magic noise?", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Capture the exact moment two partners bump heads slightly, laugh, and decide to combine their potions to create a giant, unexpected double-colored bubble explosion.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Put on a silly hat and fully embrace a goofy 'Head Wizard' persona! Use dramatic voices, encourage the kids to make loud 'WHOOSH' and 'FIZZ' sounds, and focus entirely on the joy of experimentation, mess, and play rather than neatness."}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 21:53:49.06166+00
1d8b8b27-84c8-40f2-86cc-e3a678e5fb6c	025724ee-46ad-4f3e-b37a-c573ecada70d	1	Splash & Stack Architects	An open-ended building experience where children choose how to combine water, blocks, cups, and paper to design their own unique wet-and-dry worlds.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Children select their building spot and materials. Some might build tall dry towers, some might float cups in the water, and others might construct bridges or dams using paper and blocks.", "Together": "We gather around the water tray. The teacher introduces the materials and invites the children to touch them, ask questions, and share their ideas on what they want to build first.", "Challenge": "Can you design your own play world using any mix of blocks, cups, paper, and water you choose?", "Materials": ["Plastic or foam blocks (waterproof)", "Thick construction paper or cardstock", "Plastic cups of various sizes", "Water tray filled halfway"], "Reflection": "Ask the children: 'What was your favorite choice you made today? Did you like keeping your blocks dry, or splashing them in the water? What would you build next time?'", "ChildChoice": "Children choose: 1) Where to build (inside the water, next to the water, or bridging across), 2) Which materials to mix (e.g., pouring water over blocks, making paper boats, or stacking cups), and 3) The goal of their design (making things float, balancing objects, or splashing).", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The moment a child realizes they can float a cup, stack a block on top of it, and successfully balance it in the water without it tipping over!", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Each child shares their unique creation with a peer, proudly explaining the choices they made to build their masterpiece.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "Ask questions like: 'What made you choose to put that block there? Would you like to use cups to pour water, or use them as towers? What do you think will happen if we add paper to your water bridge?'", "Try": "Try offering a challenge card only if a child seems disengaged, such as: 'Would you like to try making a boat that can carry three blocks, or would you rather build the tallest tower on dry land?'", "Watch": "Watch how children naturally choose to combine materials. Note who prefers dry, neat stacking versus wet, sensory pouring, and see how they adapt when materials change properties (like paper getting wet).", "Phases": [{"Name": "Choose and Explore", "ChildrenDo": "Touch the materials, choose their building zone (wet or dry), and pick their starting materials.", "TeacherDoes": "Observe where children gravitate, ask open-ended questions about their choices, and assist with any material distribution.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Design and Build", "ChildrenDo": "Combine blocks, cups, and paper. They test their designs, pour water, stack elements, and adjust their creations based on their own ideas.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage child autonomy. If a child's structure collapses, ask: 'What do you want to choose to try next?' instead of fixing it for them.", "DurationMinutes": 12}, {"Name": "Showcase and Test", "ChildrenDo": "Demonstrate how their creations work (e.g., pouring water through a cup tower or floating a block boat) and look at what their friends built.", "TeacherDoes": "Narrate the diverse choices made, pointing out how differently everyone used the same materials.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Cleanup and Celebrate", "ChildrenDo": "Help sort the wet materials from the dry materials and dry their hands.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead a quick celebratory high-five for a great session of building and choosing.", "DurationMinutes": 3}], "Safety": ["Ensure the floor remains dry to prevent slipping.", "Remind children to keep water inside the tray or cups."], "Closing": "We had so many different ideas today! Some of us built giant dry towers, some made floating cup-boats, and some made wet paper bridges. You are amazing architects!", "Extensions": ["Add plastic toy animals or figures so children can choose who 'lives' in their water worlds.", "Provide food coloring to let children choose a color for their water tray."], "Adaptations": ["For children who dislike wet hands, offer a 'dry-only' zone with paper and blocks.", "For fine-motor challenges, provide larger cups and bigger blocks that are easier to stack and balance."], "Preparation": ["Fill the water tray halfway with lukewarm water.", "Set up low tables or floor mats surrounding the water tray to create 'dry building zones'."], "MaterialSetup": ["Divide blocks, cups, and paper into low baskets so they are easily accessible from all sides of the play area."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Place the water tray in the center of the room with plenty of walking space around it.", "Ensure towels are nearby for quick cleanups."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Welcome, architects! Today, you are the bosses of building. We have water, cups, paper, and blocks. You get to choose: will you build a dry castle, a floating boat, a giant bridge, or something completely new? Let's decide where we want to start!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child notices that paper changes texture when wet, capture their reaction and ask them what they want to do with the soft, wet paper now.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Set up the water tray as the central hub, surrounded by easy-to-reach stations of blocks, cups, and paper. Encourage children to explore freely without a 'right' or 'wrong' way to build. Support their choices by asking how they want to use the materials rather than telling them what to make."}	Initial Creation	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 22:13:14.698792+00
cf044ef0-096a-4567-a53d-f844892bdd46	025724ee-46ad-4f3e-b37a-c573ecada70d	2	Splash & Stack Architects	An open-ended building experience where children choose how to combine water, blocks, cups, and paper to design their own unique wet-and-dry worlds.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Children select their building spot and materials. Some might build tall dry towers, some might float cups in the water, and others might construct bridges or dams using paper and blocks.", "Together": "We gather around the water tray. The teacher introduces the materials and invites the children to touch them, ask questions, and share their ideas on what they want to build first.", "Challenge": "Can you design your own play world using any mix of blocks, cups, paper, and water you choose?", "Materials": ["Plastic or foam blocks (waterproof)", "Thick construction paper or cardstock", "Plastic cups of various sizes", "Water tray filled halfway"], "Reflection": "Ask the children: 'What was your favorite choice you made today? Did you like keeping your blocks dry, or splashing them in the water? What would you build next time?'", "ChildChoice": "Children choose: 1) Where to build (inside the water, next to the water, or bridging across), 2) Which materials to mix (e.g., pouring water over blocks, making paper boats, or stacking cups), and 3) The goal of their design (making things float, balancing objects, or splashing).", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The moment a child realizes they can float a cup, stack a block on top of it, and successfully balance it in the water without it tipping over!", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Each child shares their unique creation with a peer, proudly explaining the choices they made to build their masterpiece.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "Ask questions like: 'What made you choose to put that block there? Would you like to use cups to pour water, or use them as towers? What do you think will happen if we add paper to your water bridge?'", "Try": "Try offering a challenge card only if a child seems disengaged, such as: 'Would you like to try making a boat that can carry three blocks, or would you rather build the tallest tower on dry land?'", "Watch": "Watch how children naturally choose to combine materials. Note who prefers dry, neat stacking versus wet, sensory pouring, and see how they adapt when materials change properties (like paper getting wet).", "Phases": [{"Name": "Choose and Explore", "ChildrenDo": "Touch the materials, choose their building zone (wet or dry), and pick their starting materials.", "TeacherDoes": "Observe where children gravitate, ask open-ended questions about their choices, and assist with any material distribution.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Design and Build", "ChildrenDo": "Combine blocks, cups, and paper. They test their designs, pour water, stack elements, and adjust their creations based on their own ideas.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage child autonomy. If a child's structure collapses, ask: 'What do you want to choose to try next?' instead of fixing it for them.", "DurationMinutes": 12}, {"Name": "Showcase and Test", "ChildrenDo": "Demonstrate how their creations work (e.g., pouring water through a cup tower or floating a block boat) and look at what their friends built.", "TeacherDoes": "Narrate the diverse choices made, pointing out how differently everyone used the same materials.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Cleanup and Celebrate", "ChildrenDo": "Help sort the wet materials from the dry materials and dry their hands.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead a quick celebratory high-five for a great session of building and choosing.", "DurationMinutes": 3}], "Safety": ["Ensure the floor remains dry to prevent slipping.", "Remind children to keep water inside the tray or cups."], "Closing": "We had so many different ideas today! Some of us built giant dry towers, some made floating cup-boats, and some made wet paper bridges. You are amazing architects!", "Extensions": ["Add plastic toy animals or figures so children can choose who 'lives' in their water worlds.", "Provide food coloring to let children choose a color for their water tray."], "Adaptations": ["For children who dislike wet hands, offer a 'dry-only' zone with paper and blocks.", "For fine-motor challenges, provide larger cups and bigger blocks that are easier to stack and balance."], "Preparation": ["Fill the water tray halfway with lukewarm water.", "Set up low tables or floor mats surrounding the water tray to create 'dry building zones'."], "MaterialSetup": ["Divide blocks, cups, and paper into low baskets so they are easily accessible from all sides of the play area."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Place the water tray in the center of the room with plenty of walking space around it.", "Ensure towels are nearby for quick cleanups."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Welcome, architects! Today, you are the bosses of building. We have water, cups, paper, and blocks. You get to choose: will you build a dry castle, a floating boat, a giant bridge, or something completely new? Let's decide where we want to start!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child notices that paper changes texture when wet, capture their reaction and ask them what they want to do with the soft, wet paper now.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Set up the water tray as the central hub, surrounded by easy-to-reach stations of blocks, cups, and paper. Encourage children to explore freely without a 'right' or 'wrong' way to build. Support their choices by asking how they want to use the materials rather than telling them what to make."}	Published Version	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 22:30:45.889566+00
2402212b-af12-4b7e-885e-70b6dfa565dd	a7409952-18ab-47e0-a7ab-a5b53426343f	1	Clay Monster Friends	Children roll, pat, and pinch clay to make a body, then use loose parts like pipe cleaners, googly eyes, and feathers to bring their own unique monster to life.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Roll a clay ball, pinch it into a monster body, press in colorful googly eyes, and add pipe-cleaner legs and feather wings.", "Together": "Squish and squeeze the clay together to warm it up, making funny roaring sounds as we stretch it out.", "Challenge": "Can you make a silly clay monster with lots of eyes or wiggly legs?", "Materials": ["Air-dry clay (or play dough)", "Large googly eyes", "Colorful pipe cleaners cut into 3-inch pieces", "Bright craft feathers", "Short colorful craft sticks"], "Reflection": "What does your monster love to eat? How does it walk with those legs?", "ChildChoice": "Choosing which colors of feathers to use, how many eyes to add, and where to poke the pipe-cleaner legs.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The moment a child pushes a giant googly eye into their clay lump and suddenly laughs as their clay 'comes alive' as a character.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Proudly showing off their silly monster friend to the group and giving it a funny name.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "How many eyes does your monster need? Is your monster friendly or silly?", "Try": "If the feathers or pipe cleaners keep falling out, try poking a deep hole with your finger first, then tucking the item inside and pinching the clay shut around it.", "Watch": "Observe how children use their fingers to pinch and push. Watch to ensure younger children do not put small parts in their mouths.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Warm Up & Squeeze", "ChildrenDo": "Pinch, squish, pat, and roll their clay ball on the table to warm it up.", "TeacherDoes": "Model different hand actions like rolling, clapping the clay, and squeezing, making fun sound effects.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Shape the Monster", "ChildrenDo": "Pinch and push the clay to make a base shape for their monster (flat, tall, or round).", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to try pinching 'ears' or poking 'belly buttons' into their clay.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Add Features", "ChildrenDo": "Select googly eyes, pipe cleaners, and feathers and press them firmly into the clay.", "TeacherDoes": "Support fine-motor coordination by showing children how to press items deep into the clay so they stay secure.", "DurationMinutes": 8}, {"Name": "Monster Parade", "ChildrenDo": "Gently carry or point to their clay monsters and share their names and sounds.", "TeacherDoes": "Celebrate each unique monster creation, highlighting creative choices like 'Look at those three green legs!'", "DurationMinutes": 2}], "Safety": ["Supervise closely to prevent children from putting googly eyes or small clay pieces in their mouths.", "Ensure pipe cleaner ends are not sharp."], "Closing": "Let's give our monsters a gentle wave goodbye. We will leave them on our trays to dry so they can stay strong and play another day!", "Extensions": ["Take photos of the monsters and help children write down a one-sentence story about what their monster likes to do.", "Create a simple cardboard 'home' or cave for the monsters to play in."], "Adaptations": ["For 2-year-olds: Offer larger items like craft sticks and big blocks of clay that are easier to handle.", "For 4-year-olds: Challenge them to make a family of two monsters, one big and one small."], "Preparation": ["Portion the clay into child-sized balls (about the size of an apple).", "Cut pipe cleaners into short, safe lengths.", "Sort the googly eyes, feathers, and sticks into small, accessible cups."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place one ball of clay on each child's tray.", "Put the cups of loose parts (eyes, feathers, pipe cleaners) in the center of the table so they are easy to reach."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a low table so children have plenty of elbow room to roll and press.", "Use plastic trays or placemats to define each child's workspace."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Look at this squishy clay! It is so soft and ready to play. Today, we are going to use our strong hands to make our very own silly monster friends. Let's squeeze it!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child gets excited about putting ten eyes on one side of the clay, validate their unique vision by counting the eyes together with funny blinking noises.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 20}, "TeacherGuidance": "Focus on the sensory play and exploration. For 2 and 3-year-olds, poking holes and sticking items into the clay is the main developmental goal, not creating a perfectly shaped figure."}	Initial Creation	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 22:42:59.844585+00
