s": ["For children who dislike getting wet, let them build a dry block-tower 'viewing deck' next to the water for their toy.", "For extra sensory fun, add bubble bath to the water tray for a soapy splash park."], "Preparation": ["Fill the water tray halfway with warm water.", "Gather a basket of plastic blocks, cups, and toy animals next to the tray."], "MaterialSetup": ["Drop a few cups and toys directly in the water to invite immediate play.", "Keep blocks and sponges dry in baskets nearby so kids can choose when to wet them."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Place the water tray in an easy-to-reach spot.", "Lay down towels or a plastic splash mat underneath to catch wild splashes."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Oh no! Our toy animal friends are so hot and want to go to a wild water park! But there are no rides yet! Can you help me build the silliest splash islands, slides, and pools ever? Let's make some waves!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child naturally starts making their toy talk or slide down a block, lean in and pretend to be a customer waiting in line for their amazing ride!", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy light and joyful. Act as the play coordinator—narrate the toys' adventures with fun sound effects (Wheee! Splash!) and encourage silly ways to move water, like pouring from high up or making mini waves.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 23:10:39.882758+00
69d6f666-da88-496d-8cab-51db4fe0f399	025724ee-46ad-4f3e-b37a-c573ecada70d	5	Silly Splash Island Water Park	A playful water-building adventure where children construct silly islands, wild water slides, and floating splash-zones for toy characters using cups, blocks, and water.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Children build splash zones! They stack cups to make water towers, line up blocks as slippery slides, and float cup-boats, letting their toy animals dive, slide, and splash into the water.", "Together": "We gather around the water tray. The teacher introduces some silly toy animals who 'really want to go swimming!' We practice making our best splashing sounds (Whoosh! Splash!) and talk about what rides we want to build for them.", "Challenge": "Can you build a silly splash park with slides, pools, or boats for your toy friends to play on?", "Materials": ["Water tray filled halfway", "Plastic or foam building blocks", "Plastic cups (various sizes)", "Small plastic toy animals or characters", "Sponges and small pitchers for pouring"], "Reflection": "Ask the children: 'What was your toy's favorite ride today? Who made the loudest splash sound? What should we build for our next water park adventure?'", "ChildChoice": "Children choose which toy animal to play with, whether to build a tall slide or a floating boat, and how much splashing and pouring their toy gets to do.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exact second a child slides a toy animal down a wet block ramp and it lands with a satisfying 'splat!' right inside a floating cup!", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Each child gives their toy animal one giant 'Grand Finale Splash' into their creation while making a loud, silly sound effect!", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "Ask playful questions: 'Is your toy going down the fast slide or taking a relaxing cup-bath? Can we make a rainstorm with this sponge to fill your pool?'", "Try": "Try adding a playful twist if a child is stuck: 'Oh look, this little frog is looking for a floating island! Can you build him a boat using a cup and a block?'", "Watch": "Watch for dramatic play narratives. Note how children use the physical properties of water (buoyancy, pouring, sliding) to fuel their imaginative scenarios with the toys.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Meet the Swimmers", "ChildrenDo": "Choose a toy animal, test how it floats, and splash it in the water while making funny animal noises.", "TeacherDoes": "Hand out toys, encourage silly swimming sounds, and ask children what kind of water ride their toy wants.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Build the Wild Rides", "ChildrenDo": "Stack blocks to make slides, float cups as hot tubs, and use sponges to rain water down on their toys.", "TeacherDoes": "Playfully narrate the building: 'Wow, Leo's dinosaur is climbing a giant block tower! Look at that splash!'", "DurationMinutes": 12}, {"Name": "The Grand Splash Show", "ChildrenDo": "Show off their favorite ride by sending their toy down the slide or into the pool with a big collective splash.", "TeacherDoes": "Cheer, countdown '3-2-1 Splash!', and celebrate everyone's silly ideas.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Splash-Free Cleanup", "ChildrenDo": "Squeeze water out of sponges, load toys and blocks into drying baskets, and dry their hands.", "TeacherDoes": "Make cleanup a game by asking children to rescue the toys from the water and tuck them into dry towel beds.", "DurationMinutes": 3}, {"Name": "New Phase", "ChildrenDo": "", "TeacherDoes": "", "DurationMinutes": 5}], "Safety": ["Keep the floor dry by having sponges ready to soak up spills.", "Remind children to keep splashing gentle and inside the water tray area."], "Closing": "You built the best water park ever! Our toy friends are so happy and tired from all the sliding and splashing. High-fives for our silly builders!", "Extensions": ["Add plastic funnels or bendy straws to create 'water pipes' and secret slides.", "Give children washable markers to draw swimsuits on plastic toys before they dive in."], "Adaptations": ["For children who dislike getting wet, let them build a dry block-tower 'viewing deck' next to the water for their toy.", "For extra sensory fun, add bubble bath to the water tray for a soapy splash park."], "Preparation": ["Fill the water tray halfway with warm water.", "Gather a basket of plastic blocks, cups, and toy animals next to the tray."], "MaterialSetup": ["Drop a few cups and toys directly in the water to invite immediate play.", "Keep blocks and sponges dry in baskets nearby so kids can choose when to wet them."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Place the water tray in an easy-to-reach spot.", "Lay down towels or a plastic splash mat underneath to catch wild splashes."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Oh no! Our toy animal friends are so hot and want to go to a wild water park! But there are no rides yet! Can you help me build the silliest splash islands, slides, and pools ever? Let's make some waves!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child naturally starts making their toy talk or slide down a block, lean in and pretend to be a customer waiting in line for their amazing ride!", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy light and joyful. Act as the play coordinator—narrate the toys' adventures with fun sound effects (Wheee! Splash!) and encourage silly ways to move water, like pouring from high up or making mini waves.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 23:15:01.611688+00
077fc9c2-4052-4d56-ada2-08ae4823ac70	c3adffbe-8269-4571-8dd8-978dfe4a2b2a	5	Splash & Slide: The Great Block Race!	Turn classic block play and watercolor into a high-energy, sensory painting game where blocks become colorful 'racers' leaving bright trails on a giant paper track!	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Dip different block shapes into liquid watercolor cups and slide, stamp, or hop them across giant paper sheets to paint a vibrant, messy raceway.", "Together": "We will gather around a giant paper track, dip our blocks into cups of vibrant watercolor, and race them across the paper to create a collaborative masterpiece!", "Challenge": "Can you make your block slide, stomp, or spin across the paper to leave a colorful rainbow trail?", "Materials": ["Wooden or plastic blocks of various shapes", "Large roll of butcher paper", "Plastic cups", "Water tray or shallow bin", "Liquid watercolor paint (washable, multiple colors)", "Tape", "Towels for easy cleanup"], "Reflection": "Ask the children: 'Which block made the longest slide? How did your hands feel when the blocks got slippery?'", "ChildChoice": "Children choose which block shapes to use (tall cylinders, wide rectangles, etc.), which bright watercolor cups to dip them in, and how their 'racer' moves (sliding, stomping, or spinning).", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The moment a child zooms their block fast across the paper and watches a long, bright streak of color magically appear behind it like a rocket trail!", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Stepping back together to admire a giant, collaborative roadway covered in colorful block prints, swooping slides, and rainbow textures.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What kind of track will your block racer make if it hops like a frog? What happens when your red racer crosses paths with a blue racer?", "Try": "Try sliding your block super fast to make a long streak, then try stomping it slowly like a heavy dinosaur to leave perfect shape stamps on the road.", "Watch": "Watch how children experiment with different surfaces of the blocks (flat faces vs. sharp edges) to create thick or thin painted lines, and how they naturally incorporate imaginative vehicle or animal play.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Meet the Racers", "ChildrenDo": "Choose a block racer, test how it slides on the dry paper, and practice making zooming or stomping sound effects.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrate how different shapes slide. Ask children what sounds their blocks make (e.g., 'Does your round block roll or slide?').", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "The Rainbow Slide", "ChildrenDo": "Dip the bottom of their blocks into the watercolor cups and slide, stamp, or hop them across the paper track.", "TeacherDoes": "Narrate the action like an enthusiastic sports announcer! 'Look at that green triangle zoom across the finish line! Wow, what a huge red stomp!'", "DurationMinutes": 12}, {"Name": "Splashtown Towers", "ChildrenDo": "Stack their wet, colorful blocks on the paper to build colorful towers, then slide them to wash them off in the water tray.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to stack their blocks to see how the colors mix, then guide them to wash the blocks in clean water and wipe their hands.", "DurationMinutes": 8}], "Safety": ["Ensure any spilled water or paint on the floor is wiped up immediately to prevent slipping.", "Remind children to keep the blocks sliding on the paper and not to throw them."], "Closing": "What an incredible race! Our block racers did an amazing job painting this road. Let's give our blocks a warm, bubbly bath in the sink to clean them up for their next big adventure!", "Extensions": ["Add plastic toy cars or animal figures to roll and stomp alongside the blocks.", "Let the painted paper dry and use it as a custom play-mat for toy cars and blocks the next day."], "Adaptations": ["For children sensitive to wet textures, wrap a thick rubber band around the top of the block so they have a dry handle to grip while dipping.", "For younger toddlers, use extra-large blocks that are easier to grasp with two hands."], "Preparation": ["Tape a long, wide sheet of butcher paper securely to the floor or a low table.", "Fill plastic cups halfway with water and stir in vibrant liquid watercolor paint.", "Place the color-filled cups inside the water tray to catch any splashes and spills."], "MaterialSetup": ["Set the cups of liquid watercolor inside the water tray.", "Keep damp towels nearby for quick hand-wipes during the play."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Position the water tray in the middle of the taped-down paper so children can easily reach it from all sides.", "Spread a collection of different wooden or plastic blocks around the paper area."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Welcome to the Great Block Race! Today, our blocks are going to become speedy racers. When we dip them into our magic rainbow cups, we can slide, stomp, and splash colorful tracks all over our giant paper road! Are your racers ready?", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Celebrate when a child discovers they can stamp two different shapes on top of each other to make a new design, cheering for their 'magic shape stamp'!", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy high, playful, and dramatic! Treat the blocks like characters ('Here comes speedy Blue Rectangle!'). Encourage children to make sound effects (zoom, splash, stomp) as they paint. Use large butcher paper taped securely to the floor or table to handle the energetic sliding.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 23:29:09.094016+00
