e1003485-a45e-44e5-9051-f20005c964fb	3a48a470-63cd-492c-ba6f-8dd3032205cf	5	My First Origami Sailboat	A simplified folding experience where children fold square paper to create a basic sailboat, decorate it with wax crayons, and test its ability to float in a tray of water.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Fold the bottom corner of the paper triangle up to create the flat hull of the boat, scribble colorful wax designs on it, and gently place it in a water tray to float.", "Together": "We will practice folding our paper diagonally to make a sailboat shape, then color it with wax crayons to make it waterproof.", "Challenge": "Can we fold a flat piece of paper so that it stands up like a sailboat and floats on water?", "Materials": ["Square origami paper or lightweight colored construction paper (12cm x 12cm)", "Wax crayons (essential for waterproofing)", "Shallow plastic trays filled with 2cm of water", "Small plastic figurines (lightweight, to act as passengers)", "Sponges and towels for cleanup"], "Reflection": "What kept our paper boats from sinking right away? How does the wax crayon help keep the water off the paper?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their paper color, select their crayon designs, and choose which small toy passenger gets to ride in their boat.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exciting moment when the paper boat is placed in the water and successfully floats without tipping over.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Creating a real, floating toy using simple paper-folding skills.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What happens when you blow gently on your sail? Where is your boat traveling?", "Try": "Try pressing your finger hard along the crease like a train riding on a track to make it extra flat.", "Watch": "Watch for children who might submerge the boat completely. Remind them to place it gently flat on top of the water.", "Phases": [{"Name": "The Sail Fold", "ChildrenDo": "Fold the square paper in half corner-to-corner to create a large triangle sail shape.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrates the fold, encouraging children to press hard along the folded edge to make a sharp crease.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "The Boat Hull Fold", "ChildrenDo": "Fold the bottom pointy corner of the triangle upward to create a flat, horizontal bottom edge (the boat's hull).", "TeacherDoes": "Assists children with this fold, showing how it creates a flat base that allows the boat to stand up.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Waterproofing with Wax", "ChildrenDo": "Color the bottom fold of the boat heavily with wax crayons to coat the paper.", "TeacherDoes": "Explains how the wax crayon acts like an umbrella, keeping the water away from the paper.", "DurationMinutes": 7}, {"Name": "The Float Test", "ChildrenDo": "Gently place their sailboat in the water tray and watch it float. Place a tiny plastic toy inside the fold.", "TeacherDoes": "Monitors the water play, encouraging children to gently blow on the sails to make their boats move.", "DurationMinutes": 8}], "Safety": ["Keep water depth very shallow (no more than 2-3 cm).", "Have towels ready to immediately wipe up any water spills on the floor to prevent slipping."], "Closing": "Help children rescue their boats from the water, dry them gently with a towel, and place them on a tray to take home.", "Extensions": ["Add a drop of blue food coloring to the water trays to make a realistic ocean.", "Use a straw to blow air and race the boats across the water tray."], "Adaptations": ["For younger children, use thicker pre-waxed paper (like patty wrappers) that is easier to fold and naturally waterproof.", "For older children, challenge them to fold a double-sail boat by folding both side corners upward."], "Preparation": ["Fill shallow plastic trays with a small amount of water and set them in the center of the tables.", "Set out towels nearby for wet hands."], "MaterialSetup": ["Provide each child with one square of paper and a small basket of wax crayons.", "Keep the water trays covered or out of reach until the folding and coloring are finished."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Set up the workspace near a sink or on a water-safe surface.", "Ensure clear division between dry folding areas and wet testing areas."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Ahoy, sailors! Today we are going to fold our very own sailboats out of paper. We will use magic wax crayons to make them waterproof so they can float in our miniature ocean!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Capture the look of wonder when a child realizes their paper boat doesn't melt in the water because of their heavy crayon coloring.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Model how to make a firm crease by pressing down with the side of a thumb. Emphasize that coloring the bottom of the boat heavily with crayon acts like a shield against the water.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-09 12:03:04.256179+00
