026-10-09 13:54:21.441684+00
162fa576-fa77-4ed7-ac30-583c46c0d9e9	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	5	Leaping Lily Pad Math Quest	Children decorate pre-folded leaping origami paper frogs and practice finger pressure control to make them leap across numbered lily pads.	{"AgeMax": 4, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "A custom origami frog and a lily pad target course marked with numbers and colors.", "Together": "We decorate our paper frogs with colorful patterns and test how pressing the back of the frog makes it launch into the air.", "Challenge": "Can you make your paper frog jump from lily pad to lily pad to reach the big pond?", "Materials": ["Pre-cut square paper (green, yellow, light blue)", "Pre-folded paper jumping frogs", "Green foam or felt lily pads cutouts with numbers 1-5", "Washable markers", "Googly eyes and glue dots", "Large blue floor mat or blue fabric sheet"], "Reflection": "How did your frog move differently when you pressed softly compared to when you pressed hard?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their frog color, decorative stickers, and which numbered path to leap along.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exciting instant the paper frog launches into the air and lands right on a green lily pad.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Mastering the finger tap technique to send the paper frog leaping accurately onto a target.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What happens when you press the back of the frog very quickly? Which lily pad is your frog aiming for next?", "Try": "If a child struggles to make the frog leap, place your finger over theirs to guide the downward slide release motion.", "Watch": "Observe how children adjust their finger pressure and fine motor precision when trying to launch the frog.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Frog Personalization", "ChildrenDo": "Decorate pre-folded paper frogs using markers, dots, and googly eyes.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrate how to stick googly eyes on the front and praise unique color choices.", "DurationMinutes": 8}, {"Name": "Launch Practice", "ChildrenDo": "Practice pressing down on the rear spring fold of the frog using one finger to make it launch.", "TeacherDoes": "Guide children's finger placement on the back fold. Model light versus firm pressure.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Lily Pad Hop Game", "ChildrenDo": "Launch frogs along the numbered lily pad paths, calling out the numbers as frogs land on them.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to aim for specific numbers and celebrate every successful jump.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "Tidy & Group Reflection", "ChildrenDo": "Gather paper frogs and collect lily pads into the storage basket.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead a brief cool-down transition chant about sleepy frogs resting in the pond.", "DurationMinutes": 2}], "Safety": ["Ensure children keep small googly eyes away from their mouths.", "Keep the jumping area clear of foot traffic to prevent tripping."], "Closing": "Place all the paper frogs together on the large center lily pad for a quick group photo before transition.", "Extensions": ["Add a tape measure to compare which frog jumped the longest distance.", "Introduce addition by adding up the numbers on the lily pads where the frog lands."], "Adaptations": ["Provide larger pre-folded frogs made of thick paper for children needing bigger motor targets.", "Draw visual directional arrows on the lily pads to assist visual learners."], "Preparation": ["Pre-fold 2-3 origami jumping frogs per child using sturdy cardstock or origami paper.", "Cut out 10-15 green felt circles to serve as lily pads and write numbers 1 to 5 on them.", "Spread the blue fabric sheet on the floor to represent the pond."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place blank origami frogs, markers, googly eyes, and glue dots at tables.", "Set the pre-folded blank frogs in small trays for easy access."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a wide floor space around the blue fabric pond.", "Arrange lily pads in linear paths leading toward the center of the pond."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Look at these amazing paper frogs! When we press down gently on their backs, they hop high into the air. Let's make our own frogs and help them leap across the lily pads!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "A quiet moment occurs when a child gently places their finger on the frog, takes a focused breath, and watches it flip successfully.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Focus on the physical mechanics of pressing down on the spring-fold. Pre-fold the frogs so children can focus on decorating and playing without frustration.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-12 12:04:21.834144+00
010de0b0-e379-45d7-b499-170bd591350c	8418a6dc-5770-4e75-afc5-c1fb2711a448	1	The Magical Animal Dance Party!	An active, highly playful music and movement adventure where children use colorful ribbon wands and balloons to dance like magical animals and play a thrilling freeze-dance game.	{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "Children will dance dynamically with ribbon wands and balloons, matching their body movements to fast, slow, and silly music tempos.", "Together": "We will gather in a circle, shake out our 'silly bones' together, and practice making giant wind spirals in the air using our colorful ribbon wands.", "Challenge": "Can you keep your balloon buddy floating in the air using only your elbows, knees, or your colorful ribbon wand while the music plays?", "Materials": ["Colorful ribbon wands (one per child)", "Inflated colorful balloons (plus 3-4 spares)", "Music player with pre-selected fast, slow, and silly instrumental tracks"], "Reflection": "Which animal dance made you laugh the most? How did your ribbon wand move when the music got super-fast?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their ribbon wand color, their balloon buddy, and get to invent their own silly animal dance move for the whole class to copy.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The 'Freeze-Dance Balloon Catch' where the music suddenly stops and everyone must instantly freeze while balancing their balloon on a silly body part, like their head or nose!", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "We successfully guided our balloon buddies through the magical dance forest and celebrated with a grand finale dance explosion!", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "Ask: 'How does a giant, heavy dinosaur dance compared to a tiny, bouncy frog? Can you show me with your ribbon wand?'", "Try": "Try prompting the children to tap their balloons using only their elbows, shoulders, or heads to ramp up the physical comedy and laughter.", "Watch": "Watch for children who get overstimulated or frustrated if their balloon floats away, and step in to gently toss it back into their play space to keep the play joyful.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Silly Bone Warm-Up", "ChildrenDo": "Shake out hands, feet, hips, and noses to silly sound effects, warming up their muscles.", "TeacherDoes": "Leads the movements, calling out funny body parts to shake and making silly vocal sound effects.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Ribbon Wand Windstorms", "ChildrenDo": "Select a ribbon wand, space out, and paint the air with giant loops, circles, and zig-zags matching the speed of the music.", "TeacherDoes": "Changes the music speed and narrates the play: 'Oh, now we are slow-motion sloths! Now we are speedy cheetahs!'", "DurationMinutes": 7}, {"Name": "Balloon Buddy Freeze Dance", "ChildrenDo": "Receive a balloon, tap it gently into the air to keep it up, and instantly freeze in a silly pose when the music stops.", "TeacherDoes": "Controls the music, calls out wacky freeze challenges like 'Freeze and balance your balloon on your knee!'", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "The Balloon Pop Cool-Down", "ChildrenDo": "Pretend to be balloons inflating with a deep breath, then slowly hiss and melt all the way down to the floor like deflating balloons.", "TeacherDoes": "Guides deep breathing exercises with visual balloon analogies to transition children back to a calm state.", "DurationMinutes": 3}], "Safety": ["Actively monitor for popped balloon pieces and immediately discard them to prevent choking hazards.", "Ensure children maintain a safe bubble of space around themselves to avoid ribbon wands hitting peers."], "Closing": "Give your balloon buddy a gentle high-five, put your ribbon wands back in the sleeping basket, and float quietly back to your spots like gentle clouds.", "Extensions": ["Add a bubble machine during the fast-tempo phase to create an extra sensory, magical chasing element to the dance party."], "Adaptations": ["For children with limited mobility, tie a balloon to their wrist with a soft yarn strand so they can easily participate in keeping it afloat from a seated position."], "Preparation": ["Inflate all balloons before the children arrive.", "Set up a playlist with clear shifts between fast tempos, slow tempos, and sudden pauses."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place ribbon wands in a basket on one side of the room.", "Keep the inflated balloons hidden inside a large bag or net to reveal as a exciting surprise."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a large, open floor space free of tables, chairs, and tripping hazards."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Welcome to the Magical Dance Forest, explorers! Today our bodies are turning into silly, dancing animals. Look what I have in my magic basket to help us dance!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Catch a child giggling while intensely concentrating on balancing a balloon on their forehead, and mimic their pose to share a joyful, silent connection.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep your energy high, warm, and highly playful! Model goofy, exaggerated movements to show children it is safe to be silly. Use suspenseful pauses when stopping the music to keep engagement locked in.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-09 15:17:35.07762+00
