ound, poke a deep finger hole into the clay together, and pinch it tight while saying '1, 2, 3, stick!'", "Watch": "Watch for children giggling and engaging in pretend play. Keep an eye out to make sure small googly eyes and pom-poms stay on the trays and out of mouths.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Wake Up & Splat!", "ChildrenDo": "Tickle, squeeze, and gently splat their clay on the tray to warm it up, making silly 'wake up' sounds.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead the stretching and squeezing with funny sound effects like 'streeeetch' and 'splat!'.", "DurationMinutes": 6}, {"Name": "The Magic Reveal", "ChildrenDo": "Lift up their surprise cups to discover the eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and feathers hidden underneath.", "TeacherDoes": "Gasp in excitement and encourage children to name the colorful treasures they found under their 'surprise hats'.", "DurationMinutes": 4}, {"Name": "Build a Goofy Friend", "ChildrenDo": "Press eyes, zig-zag legs, and feathers into their clay to build a silly monster face and body.", "TeacherDoes": "Circulate and make funny 'boop' and 'pop' noises as kids press items into the clay.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "The Monster Dance Party!", "ChildrenDo": "Bounce their finished monsters on the table dance floor, making them wiggle and make silly noises.", "TeacherDoes": "Play some upbeat, playful background music and narrate the funny dance moves the monsters are doing.", "DurationMinutes": 5}], "Safety": ["Ensure pipe cleaner ends are tucked or folded so they are not sharp.", "Actively supervise to prevent any mouthing of the small googly eyes or pom-poms."], "Closing": "Let's tuck our tired monsters into their trays for a nap. Let's whisper, 'Goodnight, silly monsters, thank you for dancing with us!'", "Extensions": ["Create a simple 'Monster Freeze Dance' game where children freeze their clay monsters when the music stops.", "Provide cardboard boxes as 'monster cars' to slide them around the room."], "Adaptations": ["For younger toddlers: Focus purely on poking big craft sticks and giant eyes into the clay to make 'peek-a-boo' faces.", "For older children: Challenge them to make a monster that can balance on one giant wiggly leg."], "Preparation": ["Roll the clay into round 'sleeping' balls.", "Bend several pipe cleaners into zig-zags and spirals.", "Hide the eyes, feathers, and pom-poms under plastic cups on each tray like secret surprise hats."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place a 'sleeping' ball of clay on each tray.", "Place the upside-down surprise cups next to the clay."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Create a 'Monster Dance Floor' by taping a bright sheet of paper in the center of the table.", "Set up individual trays for each child to contain the playdough splats."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Shh... look! Our clay balls are fast asleep! On the count of three, let's give them a gentle tickle and shout 'WAKE UP, CLAY!' One, two, three... WAKE UP!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child puts an eye in a weird place like the bottom of the clay, dramatically pretend the monster is looking at your toes and giggle together about its 'toe-vision'.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy high and full of pretend play. Use your voice to bring the clay to life—giggle when you touch it, make 'boop' sounds when adding eyes, and encourage children to talk to their monsters. Focus on the joy of creation rather than making a perfect figure.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:19:44.111307+00
