COPY public.platform_experiences (id, title, description, category, age_range_min_months, age_range_max_months, duration_minutes, learning_objectives, required_materials, instructions, safety_notes, dna_payload, pricing_model, price_amount, currency, publication_status, ownership_type, current_version, license_id, source_harvest_request_id, source_experience_id, source_organization_id, creator_attribution, created_at, updated_at) FROM stdin;
a0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	Forest Sensory Scavenger Hunt	Children explore outdoor textures, leaves, pinecones, and bark to build tactile vocabulary and sensory awareness.	Nature	36	60	30	\N	\N	Take children into the garden or nature area with collection pouches. Prompt them to discover smooth, rough, crunchy, and soft items.	Supervise small items to prevent choking hazards; check area for nettles or thorny bushes.	{"materials":["magnifying glass","cloth pouches","nature card"]}	IncludedInSubscription	0.00	INR	Published	CuratedLicensed	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	Little Stars Daycare Educators	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00
a0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002	Mini Engineers: Balance & Bridges	Hands-on construction activity exploring balance, weight distribution, and team cooperation with recycled wood and blocks.	STEM	48	72	40	\N	\N	Guide children to construct a bridge between two chairs that can hold a toy animal. Test with light and heavier objects.	Ensure wooden blocks have no splinters; keep building height below child eye level.	{"materials":["foam blocks","planks","toy cars"]}	OneTimePurchase	12.50	INR	Published	CuratedLicensed	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	Scandinavian Early Learning Labs	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00
a0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Watercolor Salt Textures	Creative art exploration observing how table salt interacts with wet watercolor paint to create crystallization patterns.	Art	36	60	25	\N	\N	Provide heavy watercolor paper. Paint vibrant washes, then sprinkle coarse salt. Watch crystals form as it dries.	Use non-toxic watercolors; wash hands after handling coarse salt.	{"materials":["watercolor paper","paint trays","coarse salt"]}	IncludedInSubscription	0.00	INR	Published	PlatformOwned	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	EnjoyEveryday Creative Studio	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00	2026-10-15 21:37:56.335084+00
f672f2fb-cc8a-404d-82d0-e03e5b28b56c	The Wiggle-Wobble Clay Monster Party (Focus: Make It More Playful)	Children wake up sleeping lumps of clay with silly tickles and splats, then transform them into goofy, wiggly monster friends using shaky googly eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and fluffy tickle-feathers. Adapted with emphasis on: Make it more playful.	Early Childhood	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"AgeMax":3,"AgeMin":2,"Create":"Pinch a goofy nose, press in tons of googly eyes, add bouncy zig-zag pipe cleaner legs, and give your monster a fluffy feather tail for tickling.","Together":"Let\\u0027s pat, squeeze, and gently splat our clay on the table! We\\u0027ll tickle the clay to \\u0027wake it up\\u0027 and make funny yawning noises together.","Challenge":"Can you make a super silly, wiggly clay monster that loves to dance and make funny noises? Challenge adaptation: Make it more playful.","Materials":["Brightly colored playdough or air-dry clay","Assorted sizes of googly eyes","Colorful pipe cleaners (some pre-bent into zig-zags)","Fluffy craft feathers","Soft mini pom-poms for silly noses","Small plastic cups (to hide the craft parts under)"],"Reflection":"Show me your monster\\u0027s favorite dance move! What silly sound does it make when you tickle its nose?","Environment":"Indoor / Outdoor","MagicMoment":"When a child tickles their finished monster\\u0027s tummy with a feather and lets out a huge, shared giggle as they act out the monster\\u0027s funny voice.","GroupSizeMax":10,"GroupSizeMin":4,"Accomplishment":"Hosting a wild \\u0027Monster Dance Party\\u0027 on the table, making all our creations wiggle, shake, and shout silly words together.","ExecutionGuide":{"Ask":"What is your monster\\u0027s name? Does it make a squeaky sound or a roaring sound when it wiggles?","Try":"If a monster\\u0027s wiggly leg falls off, make a dramatic \\u0027Oh no!\\u0027 sound, poke a deep finger hole into the clay together, and pinch it tight while saying \\u00271, 2, 3, stick!\\u0027","Watch":"Watch for children giggling and engaging in pretend play. Keep an eye out to make sure small googly eyes and pom-poms stay on the trays and out of mouths.","Phases":[{"Name":"Wake Up \\u0026 Splat!","ChildrenDo":"Tickle, squeeze, and gently splat their clay on the tray to warm it up, making silly \\u0027wake up\\u0027 sounds.","TeacherDoes":"Lead the stretching and squeezing with funny sound effects like \\u0027streeeetch\\u0027 and \\u0027splat!\\u0027.","DurationMinutes":6},{"Name":"The Magic Reveal","ChildrenDo":"Lift up their surprise cups to discover the eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and feathers hidden underneath.","TeacherDoes":"Gasp in excitement and encourage children to name the colorful treasures they found under their \\u0027surprise hats\\u0027.","DurationMinutes":4},{"Name":"Build a Goofy Friend","ChildrenDo":"Press eyes, zig-zag legs, and feathers into their clay to build a silly monster face and body.","TeacherDoes":"Circulate and make funny \\u0027boop\\u0027 and \\u0027pop\\u0027 noises as kids press items into the clay.","DurationMinutes":10},{"Name":"The Monster Dance Party!","ChildrenDo":"Bounce their finished monsters on the table dance floor, making them wiggle and make silly noises.","TeacherDoes":"Play some upbeat, playful background music and narrate the funny dance moves the monsters are doing.","DurationMinutes":5}],"Safety":["Ensure pipe cleaner ends are tucked or folded so they are not sharp.","Actively supervise to prevent any mouthing of the small googly eyes or pom-poms."],"Closing":"Let\\u0027s tuck our tired monsters into their trays for a nap. Let\\u0027s whisper, \\u0027Goodnight, silly monsters, thank you for dancing with us!\\u0027","Extensions":["Create a simple \\u0027Monster Freeze Dance\\u0027 game where children freeze their clay monsters when the music stops.","Provide cardboard boxes as \\u0027monster cars\\u0027 to slide them around the room."],"Adaptations":["For younger toddlers: Focus purely on poking big craft sticks and giant eyes into the clay to make \\u0027peek-a-boo\\u0027 faces.","For older children: Challenge them to make a monster that can balance on one giant wiggly leg."],"Preparation":["Roll the clay into round \\u0027sleeping\\u0027 balls.","Bend several pipe cleaners into zig-zags and spirals.","Hide the eyes, feathers, and pom-poms under plastic cups on each tray like secret surprise hats."],"MaterialSetup":["Place a \\u0027sleeping\\u0027 ball of clay on each tray.","Place the upside-down surprise cups next to the clay."],"EnvironmentSetup":["Create a \\u0027Monster Dance Floor\\u0027 by taping a bright sheet of paper in the center of the table.","Set up individual trays for each child to contain the playdough splats."],"TeacherIntroduction":"Shh... look! Our clay balls are fast asleep! On the count of three, let\\u0027s give them a gentle tickle and shout \\u0027WAKE UP, CLAY!\\u0027 One, two, three... WAKE UP!","LittleMomentOpportunity":"When a child puts an eye in a weird place like the bottom of the clay, dramatically pretend the monster is looking at your toes and giggle together about its \\u0027toe-vision\\u0027.","SuggestedDurationMinutes":25},"TeacherGuidance":"Keep the energy high and full of pretend play. Use your voice to bring the clay to life\\u2014giggle when you touch it, make \\u0027boop\\u0027 sounds when adding eyes, and encourage children to talk to their monsters. Focus on the joy of creation rather than making a perfect figure.","DevelopmentalGoals":[]}	IncludedInSubscription	0.00	INR	Draft	CuratedLicensed	1	4d88e9d7-60d6-4bc2-9e88-5babf43fdc8f	eea60dd8-7d83-44e2-be6c-bff4e67acdb0	a7409952-18ab-47e0-a7ab-a5b53426343f	20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	\N	2026-10-15 21:59:31.699268+00	2026-10-15 21:59:31.699268+00
